B.acV=s(["Initiate low-flow nasal prongs oxygen at 0.5 L/min","Start CPAP at 5\u20136 cm H\u2082O with blended oxygen titrated to SpO\u2082 91\u201395%","Administer prophylactic surfactant via endotracheal tube immediately","Intubate and initiate mechanical ventilation"],t.s)
B.a1u=new A.bV("rd_case_1","What is the most appropriate initial respiratory management?",B.acV,1,"For any neonate with GA \u226434 weeks presenting with ANY respiratory distress (regardless of whether SAS is mild or severe), the algorithm mandates starting CPAP at 5\u20136 cm H\u2082O with blended oxygen. Low-flow nasal prongs oxygen is reserved only for GA >34 weeks with mild RD.","Preterm respiratory support","A 31-week preterm infant born in a primary care center presents with nasal flaring and mild intercostal retractions 15 minutes after birth. The Silverman\u2013Andersen Score (SAS) is calculated as 3.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), START CPAP; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q11')
B.acO=s(["Start CPAP at 7 cm H\u2082O immediately","Administer an IV bolus of caffeine citrate","Start nasal prongs oxygen at 0.5\u20131 L/min and titrate to SpO\u2082 91\u201395%","Order an urgent blood gas and chest X-ray prior to initiating oxygen"],t.s)
B.a1a=new A.bV("rd_case_2","How should oxygen therapy be initiated according to the STW algorithm?",B.acO,2,"For neonates with GA >34 weeks having mild RD (SAS \u22643) and SpO\u2082 <91%, the initial step is nasal prongs oxygen at 0.5\u20131 L/min, titrating FiO\u2082 to achieve SpO\u2082 91\u201395%. Routine lab tests or X-rays are not indicated for uncomplicated mild RD.","Term mild respiratory distress","A 38-week term newborn exhibits a respiratory rate of 66/min and mild subcostal retractions (SAS = 2). Pulse oximetry on room air reads 88%.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), NASAL PRONGS OXYGEN; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q12')
B.afj=s(["Wait for established CPAP failure before intervening","Administer early rescue surfactant promptly","Increase CPAP pressure to 10 cm H\u2082O","Wait for 2 hours to administer surfactant"],t.s)
B.a1y=new A.bV("rd_case_3","What is the next best clinical action?",B.afj,1,"In preterm neonates <34 weeks with suspected worsening RDS, requiring FiO\u2082 >0.30 on CPAP \u22656 cm H\u2082O to maintain target saturation is a direct indication for early rescue surfactant, which should be given promptly before CPAP failure occurs.","Early rescue surfactant","A 29-week infant with RDS is managed on CPAP at 6 cm H\u2082O. Over the last 30 minutes, the infant's oxygen requirement has progressively escalated from FiO\u2082 0.25 to 0.35 to keep SpO\u2082within 91\u201395%. Circuit and prong placements are verified.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), EARLY RESCUE SURFACTANT; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q13')
B.ahu=s(["Increase nasal prongs oxygen flow to 4 L/min","Initiate CPAP, check prongs/circuit, and optimize pressure up to 7\u20138 cm H\u2082O","Administer routine IV fluid bolus and broad-spectrum antibiotics","Discontinue oxygen and start oral bolus feeding"],t.s)
B.a1w=new A.bV("rd_case_4","What is the correct management escalation?",B.ahu,1,"When a baby on nasal prongs oxygen shows rising oxygen requirement or worsening distress (SAS \u22654), the protocol directs escalating to CPAP, checking the circuit/prongs, increasing pressure up to 7\u20138 cm H\u2082O, and titrating FiO\u2082.","Escalating respiratory support","A 36-week infant receiving nasal prongs oxygen at 1 L/min develops increasing intercostal retractions, grunting, and rising oxygen demand, with SAS increasing from 2 to 5.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), OPTIMIZE RESPIRATORY SUPPORT; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q14')
B.agG=s(["Continue CPAP 8 cm H\u2082O and re-evaluate in 4 hours","Recognize CPAP failure, escalate support (intubate/ventilate when indicated), stabilize, and refer urgently","Reduce CPAP pressure to 4 cm H\u2082O to rest the respiratory muscles","Switch to unmonitored supplemental oxygen via nasal prongs"],t.s)
B.a1o=new A.bV("rd_case_5","What urgent action is mandated?",B.agG,1,"Persistent hypoxaemia/rising FiO\u2082 despite optimized CPAP, recurrent apnoea, shock, or fatigue defines CPAP failure. Guidelines require escalating support, intubating/ventilating when indicated, stabilizing, and referring urgently without delay.","CPAP failure and referral","A 30-week neonate on CPAP 8 cm H\u2082O and FiO\u2082 0.50 experiences recurrent severe apnoeic spells, persistent hypoxaemia (SpO\u2082 83%), and marked chest wall fatigue. Pneumothorax is ruled out by transillumination.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), CPAP FAILURE \u2014 ESCALATE PROMPTLY; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q15')
B.ae1=s(["Increase the water level in the bottle above the target PEEP line","Check for circuit air leaks, verify snug fit of binasal prongs, and check gas flow","Immediately escalate FiO\u2082 to 1.0 without checking the circuit","Increase the PEEP"],t.s)
B.a1e=new A.bV("rd_case_6","What is the most appropriate immediate step to troubleshoot this setup?",B.ae1,1,"Continuous bubbling in the generator bottle confirms delivered PEEP. If bubbling stops or distress worsens, the primary troubleshooting steps are inspecting for circuit leaks, ensuring proper sizing/seal of binasal prongs, and verifying gas flow.","Bubble CPAP troubleshooting","A staff nurse in the SNCU is setting up a bubble CPAP system for a 30-week preterm newborn with respiratory distress. After positioning the binasal prongs and starting gas flow, the nurse notices that there is no continuous bubbling in the water generator bottle and the infant's chest retractions are worsening.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), DOs; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q16')
B.a9F=s(["Apnea of prematurity","Sepsis","Normal physiological adaptation","Congenital pulmonary airway malformation"],t.s)
B.a0I=new A.bV("rd_case_7","According to the STW algorithm, what underlying complication should be actively considered at this stage?",B.a9F,1,'In the algorithm under "NOT IMPROVING / WORSENING" (characterized by persisting/increasing SAS, SpO\u2082 <91% or rising oxygen demand, and recurrent apnoea/bradycardia), the protocol explicitly instructs clinicians to "Consider sepsis (Refer to sepsis STW)".',"Worsening respiratory distress","A 30-week preterm neonate with respiratory distress was started on Bubble CPAP at birth. At 6 hours of life, despite circuit verification, the baby exhibits persistent SAS, rising oxygen demand (FiO\u2082 increased from 0.25 to 0.45), and new-onset recurrent apnoea/bradycardia episodes.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), NOT IMPROVING / WORSENING; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q17')
B.aaG=s(["Keep the baby strictly NPO on IV fluids until completely weaned off CPAP","Initiate orogastric (OG) tube feeds when appropriate, ensuring stomach decompression","Start direct oral bottle feeding immediately","Provide rapid bolus feeds via nasogastric tube without venting"],t.s)
B.a1r=new A.bV("rd_case_8","According to the STW, what is the most appropriate feeding management strategy?",B.aaG,1,"When stable on CPAP, orogastric (OG) tube feeds are indicated when appropriate, along with stomach decompression to vent swallowed air. Withholding feeds and giving IV fluids is reserved for severe RD, recurrent apnoea, poor perfusion, or abdominal concerns.","Feeding on CPAP","A 31-week preterm infant with RDS is stable on CPAP at 5 cm H\u2082O and FiO\u2082 0.25, demonstrating comfortable breathing and an SAS of 2.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), DOs; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q18')
B.abJ=s(["SAS = 4 (Mild Respiratory Distress)","SAS = 5 (Moderate\u2013Severe Respiratory Distress)","SAS = 6 (Moderate\u2013Severe Respiratory Distress)","SAS = 8 (Severe Respiratory Distress)"],t.s)
B.a1s=new A.bV("rd_case_9","What is the calculated SAS score, and how is the severity classified?",B.abJ,2,"Upper chest lag = 1; Marked lower chest retractions = 2; Just visible xiphoid retractions = 1; Minimal nares dilatation = 1; Grunt heard with stethoscope = 1. Total SAS = 6 / 10 (SAS \u22654 indicates Moderate\u2013Severe Respiratory Distress, requiring CPAP).","Silverman\u2013Andersen Score","A 32-week preterm newborn presents 20 minutes after birth with respiratory distress. Physical evaluation reveals:\n\u2022 Upper chest: Lag on inspiration\n\u2022 Lower chest: Marked intercostal retractions\n\u2022 Xiphoid retractions: Just visible indentation\n\u2022 Nares dilatation: Minimal nasal flaring\n\u2022 Expiratory grunt: Heard only with a stethoscope over the chest",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), SILVERMAN\u2013ANDERSEN SCORE; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q19')
B.ai9=s(["Calculate the Silverman\u2013Andersen Score before taking any action","Stabilize immediately (maintain TABC, provide warmth) and ventilate when indicated","Administer IV caffeine citrate bolus prior to airway assessment","Order an emergency chest X-ray and arterial blood gas"],t.s)
B.a0W=new A.bV("rd_case_10",'According to the STW "IMMEDIATE ACTIONS", what is the priority?',B.ai9,1,'Under "IMMEDIATE ACTIONS", if a baby presents with apnoea/gasping, poor effort, or shock, the protocol specifies: "Stabilize immediately; ventilate when indicated. Maintain TABC; provide warmth; admit to SNCU/NICU".',"Immediate stabilization","A newborn presents at delivery with gasping, poor respiratory effort, and signs of shock.",'ICMR/DHR STW "Respiratory Distress in Neonates" (August 2026), IMMEDIATE ACTIONS; MCQs RD.docx Section 2 Q20')
B.acz=s([B.a1u,B.a1a,B.a1y,B.a1w,B.a1o,B.a1e,B.a0I,B.a1r,B.a1s,B.a0W],t.tw)
